### 1) Simple + functional `@utility` example

```css
/* app.css */
@import "tailwindcss";

/* Simple custom utility */
@utility content-auto {
  content-visibility: auto;
}

/* Functional custom utility */
@theme {
  --tab-size-2: 2;
  --tab-size-4: 4;
}

@utility tab-* {
  tab-size: --value(--tab-size-*); /* yields tab-2, tab-4, ... */
}
```

```html
<div class="content-auto hover:content-auto md:content-auto">
  Simple custom utility with hover + responsive variants.
</div>

<div class="tab-2 hover:tab-4 md:tab-4">
  Functional utility where argument is part of the class name.
</div>
```

### How variants apply to custom utilities (from docs)

- **`hover:`**: you can prefix your custom utility class (`hover:content-auto`, `hover:tab-4`) because custom utilities are first-class utilities that “work with variants” (`hover`, `focus`, `lg`).  
- **`md:`**: behaves like any other responsive variant—`md:` applies at the medium breakpoint and above (mobile-first model), so `md:content-auto` / `md:tab-4` only affects those larger widths.

