Using the local Tailwind CSS v4 docs, custom utilities are registered with the `@utility` directive and “work with variants like `hover`, `focus` and `lg`.” The docs also say custom utilities are automatically inserted into the `utilities` layer with built-in utilities.

### Simple custom utility

```css
@utility content-auto {
  content-visibility: auto;
}
```

```html
<div class="content-auto">
  <!-- ... -->
</div>
```

### Functional utility

Functional utilities use a wildcard name like `tab-*` and resolve the argument with `--value()`:

```css
@theme {
  --tab-size-github: 8;
}

@utility tab-* {
  tab-size: --value(--tab-size-*, integer, [integer]);
}
```

This can match classes such as:

```html
<div class="tab-github tab-4 tab-[76]">
  <!-- ... -->
</div>
```

### How variants apply

Because utilities registered with `@utility` work with Tailwind variants, you can prefix them the same way as built-in utilities:

- `hover:content-auto` applies the custom utility on hover.
- `md:content-auto` applies it at the `md` responsive breakpoint.
- The same applies to functional utilities, e.g. `hover:tab-4` or `md:tab-github`.

